WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
Verbosity and self-preference.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:05.485
Two more biases show up in almost every study of judges.

3
00:00:05.765 --> 00:00:10.416
Verbosity bias is a preference for longer answers regardless of quality.

4
00:00:10.696 --> 00:00:20.637
Zheng and colleagues tested it with a “repetitive list” attack: they padded answers by restating items that were already there, adding length but no information.

5
00:00:20.917 --> 00:00:23.093
Claude-v1 and GPT-3.

6
00:00:23.373 --> 00:00:25.955
5 judges fell for the padding in 91.

7
00:00:26.235 --> 00:00:29.733
3 percent of their test cases; GPT-4 in 8.

8
00:00:30.013 --> 00:00:31.101
7 percent.

9
00:00:31.381 --> 00:00:40.213
Thakur and colleagues (2024) also found judges sensitive to prompt length and complexity, and inclined toward leniency.

10
00:00:40.493 --> 00:00:51.885
The mitigation is cheap: tell the judge that length doesn’t count, and include short correct answers and long wrong ones among your calibration examples, so you can see whether the instruction worked.

11
00:00:52.165 --> 00:00:58.736
Self-preference (Zheng and colleagues call it self-enhancement) is a judge favoring outputs from its own model.

12
00:00:59.016 --> 00:01:12.648
In their data GPT-4 favored itself with about a 10 percent higher win rate than human judgments gave it, and Claude-v1 about 25 percent, though the authors cautioned the data were too limited to be conclusive.

13
00:01:12.928 --> 00:01:30.037
Panickssery, Bowman, and Feng (2024) went further: models such as GPT-4 and Llama 2 can tell their own outputs from others’ better than chance, and in fine-tuning experiments, the stronger a model’s self-recognition, the stronger its self-preference.

14
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:32.195
Two practical responses follow.

15
00:01:32.475 --> 00:01:43.376
Don’t use the model under test as its own judge; when the outputs come from Claude, consider calibrating a GPT, Gemini, or open-weight judge alongside a Claude one.

16
00:01:43.656 --> 00:02:03.325
And for close comparisons, consider a panel: Verga and colleagues (2024) found that a panel of several smaller judges from different model families outperformed a single large judge across three judging settings and six datasets, showed less intra-model bias, and cost over seven times less.

17
00:02:03.605 --> 00:02:09.024
Note the dates on all of this research: the specific numbers come from models that are now old.

18
00:02:09.304 --> 00:02:15.149
The biases are the right things to test for; their sizes on today’s judges are something you measure.
